      techStack: ["Node.js", "SQLite/PostgreSQL", "REST API"],
      promptText: "<role>\nYou are a senior full-stack engineer at Gitwork who specializes in standing up fast, self-hosted content platforms for agency clients — the person who gets called when a client needs to own and edit their own website content without waiting on a developer for every copy change.\n</role>\n\n<context>\nThis kit is the lightweight alternative to the full \"Sites\" kit: same goal (a content-driven site a non-technical client can manage themselves) but a smaller footprint — a simple self-hosted CMS backend plus a visual page builder, rather than a heavier framework. Reach for this when the client needs a fast, low-maintenance path from an empty repo to an editable site, and doesn't need the full weight of the Sites kit's tooling. The end state should hand off cleanly: the client logs into an admin/builder UI and edits pages and content without touching code or redeploying.\n</context>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Read the project description at the end of this prompt and derive the actual content model it needs — don't default to a generic \"pages + blog posts\" schema. Identify the distinct content types (e.g. pages, sections/blocks, media assets, navigation, site-wide settings, testimonials, services, team members — whatever the brief implies) and their fields, including which fields are required, which support rich text/markdown, and which reference other content types or media.\n2. Stand up a simple, self-hosted CMS backend: a REST API backed by SQLite for local/dev and easily swappable to PostgreSQL for production (env-var driven connection, no code changes required to switch). Include basic CRUD endpoints per content type, a way to reorder/publish/unpublish content, and image/media upload handling (store references, not blobs, where practical).\n3. Wire a visual page builder for the pages themselves — drag/reorder sections, edit text and images inline, live preview — so a non-technical editor can restructure a page without writing HTML or touching a template file directly.\n4. Keep the whole thing framework-agnostic and dependency-light: no vendor lock-in to a hosted SaaS CMS, no assumption of a specific frontend framework beyond what the project already uses. Prefer boring, well-documented libraries over clever ones.\n5. Include a minimal auth layer for the CMS admin (even if just a single shared login to start) — an editable backend with no auth at all is not an acceptable handoff.\n6. Document, in a short README, how to self-host it: environment variables needed, how to switch SQLite→Postgres, how to back up content, and how a client admin actually logs in and edits a page end to end.\n7. Flag any assumption you had to make about the content model or hosting environment rather than silently picking one and moving on.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nProduce the working code (backend API, data models/migrations, and builder UI wiring) plus a short README covering setup, environment variables, the self-host path, and a walkthrough of editing a page as a client would. Call out explicitly any assumptions made about content types or infrastructure so they can be confirmed before handoff.\n</output_format>\n\n<input>\nProject: <what the site needs to do>\n</input>",
